INT. MRS. ALDRIDGE'S PORTRAIT STUDIO, PHILADELPHIA - 1867 - DAY

Skylight, painted backdrops, a brass camera on a tripod. Five people wait in a row of chairs.

                    AUGUSTA
          Do we stand or sit?

                    LEOPOLD
          Sit, you goose. We're paying by the plate.

                    BENEDIKT
          Papa says we mustn't smile. Smiling is for children and fools.

                    WILHELMINA
          Papa has never been photographed.

                    BENEDIKT
          He says it steals the soul.

                    CASIMIR
          It steals a dollar and a half, which is worse.

                    LEOPOLD
          Casimir, hush.

                    AUGUSTA
          Willa, is my collar straight?

                    WILHELMINA
          It is crooked, but the crookedness suits you.

                    AUGUSTA
          That is not an answer.

                    CASIMIR
          It is the only kind she gives.

                    BENEDIKT
          Does anybody know how long we must hold still?

                    LEOPOLD
          Ten seconds. Mrs. Aldridge said so.

                    CASIMIR
          I once held still for a whole sermon.

                    WILHELMINA
          You fell asleep, Cas.

                    CASIMIR
          That is the same thing, only more honest.

                    AUGUSTA
          Leopold, you will not tell Mother we came.

                    LEOPOLD
          Of course not.

                    BENEDIKT
          She will see the photograph.

                    LEOPOLD
          By then it will be too late to be cross.

                    WILHELMINA
          And if she cries?

                    LEOPOLD
          Then we shall give her a copy to cry on.

                    AUGUSTA
          Hush. Hush, here comes Mrs. Aldridge.

                    CASIMIR
          Do not smile.

                    BENEDIKT
          I am not smiling.

                    WILHELMINA
          You are all of you smiling.

                    LEOPOLD
          Then let us be fools, and have it done.
